(quotes from xanthian)
> Well, it was a bit more significant than that, and
> recognized as such at the time: he proved that the
> emphasis on _score_ as a measure of game "worth" was
> misplaced (...)

I still don't see how that proves anything, except that the current
score system is broken.  Score has a long and venerable history as a
measure of achievement in Roguelike games, I don't see why it
necessarily has to be different as such a measure in Nethack.

> The intent, and I think an
> intent achieved, was to turn people's minds to new
> ways to measure quality of play in NetHack, ways
> used in the 2004 tournament.

And yet, many of those new methods struck me as being no less arbitrary
than score, except that they were less broken.  Further, unless my
memory is grossly inaccurate, I think I remember seing a specific
notice from the /dev/null people that the tournament would resume using
score for the major award if the system were fixed.

> There is no "end game rider problem"; one can as
> easily reverse genocide trolls a few times and do
> pretty much the same thing, and there are dozens of
> other ways of achieving endless score improvements.

Trolls eventually fail to revive on their own.  Plus, the score award
for defeating a troll is much less than an endgame rider.  But the
problem is less finding an arbitrary source of points than surviving
long enough to do so, and having the patience to do it often enough to
get those absurd scores.  This is the entire reason score overhalls are
being proposed.

> What there is, is a "'score is everything' mentality
> problem", that both emphasises high score to the
> exclusion of other, better measures of merit

I don't think there is such a problem.  Score is one more measure of
game skill, and it is one that has a unique handle on comprensibility
to new players raised on video games.

It is true that score is somewhat arbitrary, and maximizing score can
become a strategy to itself outside the normal playing of the game.
You can win at Rogue, to bring that game up yet again, without getting
a really good score, simply by ignoring gold.  In fact, the game is
slightly easier if you don't worry about gold, since you don't waste
those turns seeking it out, which means less food consumption and less
chance a Big Nasty won't chance upon you before you find the down
stairs.

But anyway, I get the feeling neither of us is going to convince the
other on this matter.

